  "query_preview": "..\n(Friday night through next Thursday)\nIssued at 1132 PM CDT Wed Jun 17 2026\n\nModels have their differences in how the convection develops\nThursday night or Friday, and where any possible MCS may track.\nVery little in the upper levels to point to, and much of this will\nbe simply mesoscale driven. Temperatures on Friday wont be nearly\nas hot and air mass will not have the same extreme instability, \nso not expecting widespread severe weather. Still, if a strong \nenough cold pool can develop with a MCS then some strong to severe\nwinds may be a possibility. Otherwise, POPs from the model blends\nappears as a decent first guess at the moment (too high from some\nmodels like the HRRR, and too low from NAMNEST and RRFS). Wherever\nthe MCS develops, heavy rainfall will be a threat with PWAT values\nclimbing well above 2 inches. \n\nZonal flow prevails for the weekend, and without the passage of\nany real cold front, temperatures will rebound fairly quickly back\ninto the 90s to near 100 by Sunday, although wet soils may hinder\nthis a few degrees in those areas that see significant rainfall. \n\nNorthwest flow develops for early next week, with a weak shortwave\nmoving across the area. This favors afternoon convection across\nOklahoma and the Panahandle, with the convection dropping\nsoutheast towards the Big Country overnight. Thus, best POPs north\nof I-20 with much lower POPs the farther south into the I-10\ncorridor.",
